# OS / editor junk
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
.vscode/
.idea/

# Convention: anything kept local-only goes in private/
private/

# Secrets — NEVER commit AWS or model-provider credentials
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
.aws/
credentials

# Terraform — state holds resource IDs and can hold secrets in plaintext
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example
.terraform/
crash.log
crash.*.log
# NOTE: .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored. It pins provider versions and hashes and
# belongs in version control, exactly like uv.lock. Ignoring it makes provider versions
# non-reproducible between machines and CI.

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# Node / frontend
node_modules/
.next/
.vite/

# Local data artifacts — the ingestion output lives in S3 and is rebuilt from source.
# data/ is fully untracked, which also keeps it off the GitHub root listing. The tracked pointer to
# the pinned artifact version gets a home in the v0.1 IMPLEMENTATION doc; it is not this directory.
data/
*.parquet
*.sqlite3

# Eval run output — reproducible by re-running the suite.
# NOTE the trailing `/*` rather than `/`: git cannot re-include a file whose parent DIRECTORY is
# excluded, so ignoring the directory itself would make the negation below silently inert.
**/eval/results/*
eval-results/

# EXCEPT runs that re-running does not reproduce. A judge pass costs money, is the only place a measured
# judge-human agreement figure exists, and re-running it produces a different file rather than the same
# one — the judge is not deterministic at temperature 0, measured over three runs on 2026-08-21.
# `.claude/rules/evals.md` requires that agreement be "reported permanently next to every judged metric"
# — a number quoted in a doc whose evidence lives on one laptop does not satisfy that, and this repo's
# whole claim is that provenance is structural. Added 2026-08-20.
#
# EXTENDED 2026-08-24, after the rule above was found to be doing less than its comment claimed. It
# matched `-judge.json` only, so the first tier 2 run and the held-out run were both being ignored by a
# rule written to keep exactly that kind of file. Two suffixes added:
#
#   -tier2.json    judged, costs money, non-deterministic. Same argument as -judge.json, missed because
#                  the negation was written against a filename rather than against the reason.
#   -heldout.json  THE STRONGEST CASE IN THE REPO, not the weakest. Re-running the held-out set does not
#                  reproduce this file; it spends the one property the set exists to have. Losing it to a
#                  `git clean` means the phase 4 held-out result cannot be recovered at any price.
#
# Anything added here must be non-reproducible *in principle*, not merely expensive. Plain `-bedrock.json`
# runs stay ignored: they cost about 36 cents and re-running one answers the same question again.
!**/eval/results/*-judge.json
!**/eval/results/*-tier2.json
!**/eval/results/*-heldout.json

# AWS SAM / build litter
.aws-sam/
